From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: idiazraz <100824525+idiazraz@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 21:30:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] airoha: en7523: gn630v: steer LAN receive work to the second
 core

OpenWrt's packet steering places RPS by NAPI thread. airoha_eth has
none, so every Ethernet RX queue (eth0 and pon0) was steered to CPU0,
where the LAN interrupt already runs; CPU1 did almost no packet work.

A platform packet-steering.sh runs the stock script, then points the
eth0 queues at CPU1. pon0 keeps CPU0, since its interrupt is on CPU1
(gn630v-irq). Only with packet_steering=1 and on the GN630V.

On the dev board, router-local speed test over 1 Gbit, two runs each:
CPU0 softirq 88-92% -> 13-14%, CPU1 6-7% -> 85%, download 441/471 ->
544/557 Mbit/s, upload 357/329 -> 593/427 Mbit/s.
---
 .../usr/libexec/platform/packet-steering.sh     | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)
 create mode 100755 target/linux/airoha/en7523/base-files/usr/libexec/platform/packet-steering.sh

diff --git a/target/linux/airoha/en7523/base-files/usr/libexec/platform/packet-steering.sh b/target/linux/airoha/en7523/base-files/usr/libexec/platform/packet-steering.sh
new file mode 100755
index 0000000..2d4469c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/target/linux/airoha/en7523/base-files/usr/libexec/platform/packet-steering.sh
@@ -0,0 +1,17 @@
+#!/bin/sh
+# Note: OpenWrt's packet-steering.uc places RPS by NAPI thread. airoha_eth has
+# none, so it steers every Ethernet RX queue to CPU0, where the LAN interrupt
+# already runs, and CPU1 sits idle. On the GN630V send the LAN receive work to
+# CPU1; the WAN (pon0) interrupt runs on CPU1 (gn630v-irq) and stays steered
+# to CPU0.
+
+/usr/libexec/network/packet-steering.uc "$@"
+
+[ "$1" = 1 ] || exit 0
+. /lib/functions.sh
+[ "$(board_name)" = "skyworth,gn630v" ] || exit 0
+
+for q in /sys/class/net/eth0/queues/rx-*/rps_cpus; do
+	[ -e "$q" ] && echo 2 > "$q"
+done
+exit 0
